1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,845
He is thirty-three years old. He plays
the violin for a living.

2
00:00:03,845 --> 00:00:07,690
His name is Wallace Hartley. Tonight
he is on the Titanic.

3
00:00:07,690 --> 00:00:11,536
It is the night of April
fourteenth, nineteen twelve.

4
00:00:11,536 --> 00:00:15,381
He leads the ship's band. Eight men
in matching uniforms.

5
00:00:15,381 --> 00:00:19,226
Earlier, they played at dinner. Now they
play the after-supper concert.

6
00:00:19,226 --> 00:00:23,071
First-class passengers sit in leather
chairs, drinks in hand.

7
00:00:23,071 --> 00:00:26,917
A young couple dances slowly at the
edge of the room.

8
00:00:26,917 --> 00:00:30,762
The sea is glass-flat. The sky is
full of stars.

9
00:00:30,762 --> 00:00:34,607
It is the most ordinary night of the
most extraordinary voyage.

10
00:00:34,607 --> 00:00:38,452
At eleven forty, there is a soft bump
beneath their feet.

11
00:00:38,452 --> 00:00:42,297
Not loud. Not violent. Like a tray
dropped in a far-off kitchen.

12
00:00:42,297 --> 00:00:46,143
The dancers stop. The conversation
pauses. Then continues.

13
00:00:46,143 --> 00:00:49,988
Someone laughs. Probably nothing. They've
all heard worse at sea.

14
00:00:49,988 --> 00:00:53,833
Hartley does not stop playing. The band
does not stop playing.

15
00:00:53,833 --> 00:00:57,678
Outside, the ship is dying. Inside,
the music plays on.

16
00:00:57,678 --> 00:01:01,524
Below decks, the cold sea is
already pouring in.

17
00:01:01,524 --> 00:01:05,369
Stewards begin knocking on doors. Wake
up. Put on your lifebelt.

18
00:01:05,369 --> 00:01:09,214
It's only a precaution, they say.
It's nothing serious.

19
00:01:09,214 --> 00:01:13,059
Up in the lounge, the bandmaster
has been told.

20
00:01:13,059 --> 00:01:16,904
He nods. He does not change his
expression. He raises his bow.

21
00:01:16,904 --> 00:01:20,750
He chooses something cheerful. A
waltz. Something bright.

22
00:01:20,750 --> 00:01:24,595
If the passengers see them play,
they will not panic.

23
00:01:24,595 --> 00:01:28,440
That is the job. That is the
only job left.

24
00:01:28,440 --> 00:01:32,285
Some passengers move toward the
lifeboats. Some refuse to go.

25
00:01:32,285 --> 00:01:36,131
There are not enough boats. Not
nearly enough boats.

26
00:01:36,131 --> 00:01:39,976
The musicians have been told to keep
playing. They keep playing.

27
00:01:39,976 --> 00:01:43,821
After a time, the lounge begins to
empty around them.

28
00:01:43,821 --> 00:01:47,666
They move out into the foyer, then onto
the boat deck itself.

29
00:01:47,666 --> 00:01:51,512
Out in the cold air, under the stars,
they begin to play again.

30
00:01:51,512 --> 00:01:55,357
The deck is tilting now. They brace
their feet and play.

31
00:01:55,357 --> 00:01:59,202
Passengers run past them. The
musicians do not look up.

32
00:01:59,202 --> 00:02:03,047
A woman pauses. She looks at them.
Then she runs on.

33
00:02:03,047 --> 00:02:06,892
An officer asks Hartley if he wants
to try for a boat.

34
00:02:06,892 --> 00:02:10,738
Hartley shakes his head. He
raises the bow again.

35
00:02:10,738 --> 00:02:14,583
He is engaged to be married. Her
name is Maria.

36
00:02:14,583 --> 00:02:18,428
They were going to be married in the
summer. He had bought a ring.

37
00:02:18,428 --> 00:02:22,273
She is waiting for him in Lancashire.
She does not know yet.

38
00:02:22,273 --> 00:02:26,119
On the deck, the angle of the
ship gets sharper.

39
00:02:26,119 --> 00:02:29,964
Water reaches the lower decks. The
lights begin to flicker.

40
00:02:29,964 --> 00:02:33,809
The band plays on. The instruments
somehow stay in tune.

41
00:02:33,809 --> 00:02:37,654
Survivors will argue, for the rest of their
lives, what they played at the end.

42
00:02:37,654 --> 00:02:41,499
Some will say it was a hymn.
Nearer My God to Thee.

43
00:02:41,499 --> 00:02:45,345
Some will say it was a different tune
entirely. Songe d'Automne.

44
00:02:45,345 --> 00:02:49,190
What everyone agreed on was that the
band never stopped.

45
00:02:49,190 --> 00:02:53,035
By two in the morning, the deck is
too steep to stand on.

46
00:02:53,035 --> 00:02:56,880
The musicians lay down their instruments.
There is nothing more.

47
00:02:56,880 --> 00:03:00,726
Hartley straps his violin case to his body. He
does not want to be parted from it.

48
00:03:00,726 --> 00:03:04,571
There is one more thing he says. We
have done our duty.

49
00:03:04,571 --> 00:03:08,416
Then the great ship breaks. Then the
great ship is gone.

50
00:03:08,416 --> 00:03:12,261
The freezing water takes most of
them within an hour.

51
00:03:12,261 --> 00:03:16,106
Distant lifeboats hear the singing
first. Then the silence.

52
00:03:16,106 --> 00:03:19,952
When the rescue ship arrives at
dawn, the sea is empty.

53
00:03:19,952 --> 00:03:23,797
Of the eight musicians who
played, none survived.

54
00:03:23,797 --> 00:03:27,642
Hartley's body was recovered
ten days later.

55
00:03:27,642 --> 00:03:31,487
He was still wearing his uniform. His violin
case was still strapped to him.

56
00:03:31,487 --> 00:03:35,333
He was thirty-three. He had been engaged
for less than a year.

57
00:03:35,333 --> 00:03:39,178
His body was brought home to Lancashire. To
the town where he grew up.

58
00:03:39,178 --> 00:03:43,023
Thirty thousand people came to
his funeral.

59
00:03:43,023 --> 00:03:46,868
The streets were too narrow to hold them.
They stood on rooftops.

60
00:03:46,868 --> 00:03:50,713
Seven bands played the music he
had played at sea.

61
00:03:50,713 --> 00:03:54,559
Maria came. She wore mourning. She
stood at the front.

62
00:03:54,559 --> 00:03:58,404
She would never marry. She lived
another seventy years alone.

63
00:03:58,404 --> 00:04:02,249
His violin was returned to her, in
its battered case.

64
00:04:02,249 --> 00:04:06,094
She kept it for the rest of her life.
Then it disappeared.

65
00:04:06,094 --> 00:04:09,940
For almost a hundred years, no one
knew where it was.

66
00:04:09,940 --> 00:04:13,785
Then, in two thousand and six, a man
cleaning a loft found it.

67
00:04:13,785 --> 00:04:17,630
Inside was a violin, a silver plaque, and
a name engraved on it.

68
00:04:17,630 --> 00:04:21,475
Wallace Hartley. A gift from Maria. The violin
she gave him on their engagement.

69
00:04:21,475 --> 00:04:25,320
Experts authenticated it. The salt of the
Atlantic was still in the wood.

70
00:04:25,320 --> 00:04:29,166
The violin that played as the ship went
under. The actual one.

71
00:04:29,166 --> 00:04:33,011
It sold at auction for nine hundred
thousand pounds.

72
00:04:33,011 --> 00:04:36,856
It is on display today. People travel from
around the world to see it.

73
00:04:36,856 --> 00:04:40,701
They stand quietly. They take
photographs. Some of them weep.

74
00:04:40,701 --> 00:04:44,547
A wooden object that should not exist. That
refused to stay at the bottom.

75
00:04:44,547 --> 00:04:48,392
Wallace Hartley had two hours to
live. He chose to play.

76
00:04:48,392 --> 00:04:52,237
Nothing forced him. No one would have blamed
him for trying for a boat.

77
00:04:52,237 --> 00:04:56,082
He played because he chose to. Because
the music was his answer.

78
00:04:56,082 --> 00:04:59,928
The ship sank. The musicians drowned. The
cold ocean took them all.

79
00:04:59,928 --> 00:05:03,773
But the music did not. The music kept
going. The music is still here.

80
00:05:03,773 --> 00:05:07,618
Wallace Hartley had one chance to be
remembered, and he gave it away.

81
00:05:07,618 --> 00:05:11,463
He played for people he did not know. He
played to keep them calm.

82
00:05:11,463 --> 00:05:15,308
He chose dignity in a place where almost no one
would have judged him for less.

83
00:05:15,308 --> 00:05:19,154
His name became famous because of how he
ended. Not how he lived.

84
00:05:19,154 --> 00:05:22,999
Maria never married. The violin survived.
The music outlived the ship.

85
00:05:22,999 --> 00:05:26,844
He had one night to choose what kind of man
he was. He chose the music.
